[SECTION]
TITLE: Context

[TEXT]
Feature: **FILESYSTEM-BOUNDARY** — Ada.Directories bridge for anystrings.

Today, users who need filesystem access must interact with ``Ada.Directories``
directly and manually convert ``String_Type`` path values into a ``String``
via ``To_Latin_1 (Path)`` before each call.  On the ``wide``, ``wide_wide``,
``uxstring``, and ``vss`` backends this round-trip corrupts non-Latin-1 path
bytes: ``To_Latin_1`` performs a Latin-1-widening encode, not a UTF-8 decode,
so any non-ASCII byte in the path is silently replaced by a Latin-1 surrogate
or ``'?'``.  The downstream Aclida CLI ``OS.Files`` shim was written to work
around exactly this bug.

This feature adds a thin bridge so that user code can check path existence,
query filesystem entry kind, create directories, delete ordinary files, and
rename filesystem entries — all through ``String_Types``, without any
backend-specific conversion code at the call site.

All five bundled backends (``standard``, ``wide``, ``wide_wide``, ``uxstring``,
``vss``) MUST expose an identical public surface for this feature through
``String_Types``.

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: File_Kind Enumeration

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-001
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: File_Kind enumeration mirroring Ada.Directories.File_Kind
STATEMENT: >>>
The generic signature package ``Anystrings`` SHALL declare a public enumeration
type ``File_Kind`` with exactly three values: ``Directory``, ``Ordinary_File``,
and ``Special_File``, corresponding in meaning to the identically named values
of ``Ada.Directories.File_Kind`` (Ada RM A.16).

The enumeration SHALL be part of the generic signature so that all backends
expose it uniformly through ``String_Types`` without requiring consumers to
``with Ada.Directories``.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Mirroring ``Ada.Directories.File_Kind`` rather than defining independent
semantics ensures that the mapping in FUNC-DIR-003 (Kind query) is a
bijection: each ``Ada.Directories.File_Kind`` value maps to the corresponding
``String_Types.File_Kind`` value with no information loss.

The downstream pain point is the Aclida CLI ``OS.Files`` shim, which currently
re-declares an equivalent enumeration locally because there is no backend-
neutral type to import from ``String_Types``.
<<<

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Filesystem Query Operations

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-002
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Exists — total filesystem presence query
STATEMENT: >>>
The public API SHALL provide a function with the following signature:

  function Exists (Path : String_Type) return Boolean

The function SHALL return ``True`` if and only if ``Path`` resolves to an
existing filesystem entry visible to the current process.  Its semantics
SHALL be equivalent to ``Ada.Directories.Exists`` applied to the encoded form
of ``Path`` per the rule in FUNC-DIR-007.

The function SHALL be total: any runtime exception, including but not limited
to ``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Name_Error``, ``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Use_Error``,
``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Status_Error``, and ``Constraint_Error``, SHALL be caught
internally and cause the function to return ``False`` rather than propagate
to the caller.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
The totality contract is the primary design rationale.  The downstream Aclida
CLI ``OS.Files`` shim currently wraps every ``Ada.Directories`` call in an
exception handler; making the bridge total eliminates that boilerplate.

``Storage_Error`` is addressed by NFR-DIR-001, which governs the exception-
absorption policy for all five operations.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-003
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Kind — total filesystem entry kind query
STATEMENT: >>>
The public API SHALL provide a function with the following signature:

  function Kind (Path : String_Type) return File_Kind

The function SHALL return the kind of the filesystem entry at ``Path``, mapped
to the ``File_Kind`` enumeration (FUNC-DIR-001) as follows:

- ``Ada.Directories.Directory``     → ``Directory``
- ``Ada.Directories.Ordinary_File`` → ``Ordinary_File``
- ``Ada.Directories.Special_File``  → ``Special_File``

The path encoding rule of FUNC-DIR-007 applies.

The function SHALL be total: any runtime exception (including
``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Name_Error`` when the path does not exist) and any
``Ada.Directories.File_Kind`` value not listed above SHALL cause the function
to return ``Special_File`` rather than propagate an exception.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Returning ``Special_File`` as the catch-all default is consistent with Ada RM
A.16: ``Special_File`` is defined as the fallback for any entry that is
neither a directory nor an ordinary file.  Using it as the error sentinel keeps
the contract unambiguous: callers that need to distinguish a genuine
``Special_File`` from an error path should call ``Exists`` first.

The downstream pain point is identical to FUNC-DIR-002: the Aclida
``OS.Files`` shim wraps every ``Ada.Directories.Kind`` call in an exception
handler specifically to handle ``Name_Error``.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-001

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Filesystem Mutation Operations

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-004
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Create_Directory — total directory creation
STATEMENT: >>>
The public API SHALL provide a function with the following signature:

  function Create_Directory (Path : String_Type) return Boolean

The function SHALL create the directory at ``Path`` and return ``True`` if the
creation succeeded.  Its semantics SHALL be equivalent to calling
``Ada.Directories.Create_Directory`` with the encoded form of ``Path`` per
FUNC-DIR-007.

The function SHALL be total: any runtime exception, including
``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Name_Error``, ``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Use_Error``,
``Ada.IO_Exceptions.Status_Error``, and ``Constraint_Error``, SHALL be caught
internally and cause the function to return ``False`` rather than propagate
to the caller.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Returning ``Boolean`` rather than using an Ada.Directories-style procedure
(which raises on failure) follows the library's existing pattern of wrapping
every ``String``-bearing OS boundary in a total function.  The pattern was
established for the UTF-8 / Latin-1 conversion boundary and extended to the
command-line access boundary; this requirement extends it to the filesystem
boundary.

A ``False`` return covers both the error case (e.g. permission denied, path
already exists as a non-directory) and the precondition violation case (e.g.
the parent directory does not exist).  Callers that need finer-grained status
should check ``Exists`` and ``Kind`` before calling ``Create_Directory``.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-002

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-005
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Delete_File — total ordinary-file deletion
STATEMENT: >>>
The public API SHALL provide a function with the following signature:

  function Delete_File (Path : String_Type) return Boolean

The function SHALL delete the ordinary file at ``Path`` and return ``True`` if
the deletion succeeded.  Its semantics SHALL be equivalent to calling
``Ada.Directories.Delete_File`` with the encoded form of ``Path`` per
FUNC-DIR-007.

The function SHALL be total: any runtime exception SHALL be caught internally
and cause the function to return ``False`` rather than propagate to the caller.

The function is specified for ordinary files only; behaviour when ``Path``
names a directory or a special file is implementation-defined.  Callers SHOULD
verify the entry kind via FUNC-DIR-003 before calling this function.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Ada RM A.16 defines ``Delete_File`` as raising ``Name_Error`` if the entry does
not exist or is not an ordinary file, and ``Use_Error`` if the environment does
not permit deletion.  The totality contract absorbs both into a ``False``
return, consistent with FUNC-DIR-002 through FUNC-DIR-004.

The restriction to ordinary files mirrors ``Ada.Directories.Delete_File``
semantics and avoids the need to specify recursive-directory-deletion
behaviour in this requirement.  A separate ``Delete_Directory`` function may
be specified in a future requirement.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-003

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-006
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Rename — total filesystem entry rename
STATEMENT: >>>
The public API SHALL provide a function with the following signature:

  function Rename (Old_Path, New_Path : String_Type) return Boolean

The function SHALL rename the filesystem entry at ``Old_Path`` to ``New_Path``
and return ``True`` if the rename succeeded.  Its semantics SHALL be equivalent
to calling ``Ada.Directories.Rename`` with the encoded forms of both paths
per FUNC-DIR-007.

The function SHALL be total: any runtime exception SHALL be caught internally
and cause the function to return ``False`` rather than propagate to the caller.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Both path arguments are encoded independently using the same rule (FUNC-DIR-007).
The function does not guarantee atomicity of cross-device renames; that is a
POSIX / ``Ada.Directories`` implementation detail.

The totality contract covers the common failure modes: ``Name_Error`` when
``Old_Path`` does not exist, ``Use_Error`` when the environment does not permit
the rename, and ``Status_Error`` on I/O precondition violations.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-003

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Encoding Semantics

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-007
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: Path encoding — UTF-8 on Unicode backends, byte-passthrough on standard
STATEMENT: >>>
Each backend SHALL convert a ``String_Type`` path to the ``Standard.String``
expected by ``Ada.Directories`` according to the following rules:

- The ``standard`` backend SHALL pass path bytes through unchanged via
  ``To_Latin_1 (Path)``.  Because the ``standard`` backend's storage IS its
  byte stream, no re-encoding is performed.
- The ``wide``, ``wide_wide``, ``uxstring``, and ``vss`` backends SHALL
  encode ``Path`` to UTF-8 using the backend's native UTF-8 serialisation
  primitive — the inverse of the UTF-8 ingestion path defined for the command-
  line bridge by FUNC-CMDLINE-008 — before passing the result to
  ``Ada.Directories``.

No backend SHALL use ``To_Latin_1`` as the encoding step for a
Unicode-capable backend path, because that function performs Latin-1 widening
rather than UTF-8 encoding and corrupts non-Latin-1 path bytes.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This requirement documents and forbids the ``To_Latin_1`` byte-corruption bug
that motivated the entire filesystem-boundary feature: the Aclida CLI
``OS.Files`` shim was introduced specifically because calling
``To_Latin_1 (Path)`` on a ``wide_string``-backed path containing e.g. ``café``
silently replaced the UTF-8 multibyte sequence with Latin-1 surrogates, causing
``Ada.Directories`` to receive a malformed path string.

The UTF-8 encoding direction is the dual of the encoding-precise ingestion
rule established by FUNC-CMDLINE-008 for the command-line bridge.  Both rules
share the invariant: the bytes handed to the OS MUST be the UTF-8 encoding of
the Unicode scalar values stored in the ``String_Type``, not a Latin-1
representation of those scalar values.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-CMDLINE-008

[/SECTION]

[SECTION]
TITLE: Backend Coverage and API Parity

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: FUNC-DIR-008
STATUS: Approved
PRIORITY: Must
TITLE: All five backends expose identical filesystem API through String_Types
STATEMENT: >>>
The filesystem access API SHALL be accessible uniformly from
``with String_Types; use String_Types;`` for each of the five bundled backends:

- ``src/strings/standard/``   — Latin-1 / Ada ``String`` backend
- ``src/strings/wide/``       — ``Wide_String`` / ``Wide_Character`` backend
- ``src/strings/wide_wide/``  — ``Wide_Wide_String`` / ``Wide_Wide_Character``
                                backend
- ``src/strings/uxstring/``   — UXStrings backend
- ``src/strings/vss/``        — VSS backend

The public signatures for the following SHALL be identical across all five
backends such that user code written against one backend compiles and runs
correctly against any other without modification:

- The ``File_Kind`` enumeration (FUNC-DIR-001).
- The ``Exists`` function (FUNC-DIR-002).
- The ``Kind`` function (FUNC-DIR-003).
- The ``Create_Directory`` function (FUNC-DIR-004).
- The ``Delete_File`` function (FUNC-DIR-005).
- The ``Rename`` function (FUNC-DIR-006).
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
This mirrors the backend-swap guarantee that governs the rest of the
``String_Types`` surface, established by NFR-UTF8-006 for the UTF-8 operations
and extended to the command-line bridge by FUNC-CMDLINE-009.

The exact mechanism by which each ``string_types.ads`` makes these operations
available (renaming, instantiation, direct declaration) is a tech-spec decision
out of scope here.
<<<
RELATIONS: FUNC-DIR-001, FUNC-DIR-002, FUNC-DIR-003, FUNC-DIR-004, FUNC-DIR-005, FUNC-DIR-006, FUNC-CMDLINE-009

[/SECTION]
